WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:02.055
GRANT: Hey, Martin,
what are you doing?

00:00:02.055 --> 00:00:03.450
MARTIN: Hey, Grant.

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.850
We get a lot of questions
about whether one should use

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:09.000
Cloud Functions or Cloud Run.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.626
When should one use which one?

00:00:11.626 --> 00:00:15.120
GRANT: Yeah, let's
show some examples.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.359
MARTIN: Sounds great.

00:00:16.359 --> 00:00:18.275
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.586 --> 00:00:25.950
[SPARKLING SOUNDS]

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.600
CritterWatch has
come to us and asked,

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:31.800
should they use Cloud Functions
or Cloud Run for their service?

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.450
They build wildlife cameras.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.730
So you can put one of their
cameras in your backyard

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:36.900
or out in the forest.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.360
And whenever an animal passes
in front of the camera,

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.330
it takes a picture and
uploads it to the cloud.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.820
Now, that picture--
those pictures uploaded

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:46.830
to the cloud-- they
want machine learning

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:49.380
to work on those pictures,
identify what species

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:54.720
of animals were in the pictures,
and then insert these sightings

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.150
into a Firestore database.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:01.300
So, Grant, should they use
Cloud Functions or Cloud Run?

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:02.180
What do you think?

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:04.830
GRANT: Well, looking
at the selection grid,

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:08.670
they probably should
use Cloud Functions.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:11.130
I mean, they could
use Cloud Run.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:14.340
But it requires a little
bit more configuration.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.940
And with sending a Google
Cloud Storage event to Pub/Sub,

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:21.450
to Cloud Run, it's
a little bit more

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.150
complicated, where Cloud
Functions does this

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.205
automatically.

00:01:25.205 --> 00:01:26.160
MARTIN: Got it.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.480
What would the code
look like for this?

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.860
GRANT: Now, let's go
dive into VS code.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:31.572
MARTIN: Yeah.

00:01:31.572 --> 00:01:33.030
So here's the code
for the function

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:34.540
that analyzes the image.

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:38.481
Now, Grant, what happens here in
the beginning of the function?

00:01:38.481 --> 00:01:40.830
GRANT: Yeah, so we're
going to set up the Vision

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:43.830
API and the Firestore API.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.140
And then we're going
to go and track

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.980
a couple of the animals, things
you might see in your backyard,

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:55.750
like raccoons, alpacas,
peacocks, dogs, geckos.

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:57.930
MARTIN: Wow, your backyard
is a lot more exciting

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:00.570
than mine, Grant.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:05.680
GRANT: And then we'll go
and define a function.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.169
So this is triggered whenever
a Cloud Storage bucket--

00:02:10.169 --> 00:02:12.600
there's a change there.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.610
MARTIN: Oh, so
we, as developers,

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.380
don't call this function.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.240
Google Cloud Platform calls it.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:19.620
Is that right?

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:22.380
GRANT: Yes, it detects
the event automatically

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.740
and calls this function.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:27.740
MARTIN: When-- and
the event being--

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:30.350
GRANT: So whenever a
new file is uploaded

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.620
to this particular bucket.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:35.150
MARTIN: OK, so that is, a
camera out there in the field

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:37.830
uploads an image.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:38.330
Got it.

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:41.486
And then what does this
function do with the image?

00:02:41.486 --> 00:02:44.050
GRANT: So then with
the image event,

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:51.770
it goes and uses the Cloud
Vision API against that file.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:56.720
And with the result, it
returns a JSON object

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:02.970
with a bunch of different labels
of what it finds in the image.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.330
So it could be things
that aren't animals.

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:07.528
But we only really
care about the animals.

00:03:07.528 --> 00:03:08.570
MARTIN: Oh, that's right.

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:11.870
It could be a fence, or lawn
furniture, or things like that.

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:13.370
But it seems like we extract--

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:14.450
GRANT: Yes, like cars.

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:17.820
MARTIN: Car-- right, we extract
only the animal tags here.

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:18.320
Cool.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.860
And then it looks
like down on line 30,

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:24.860
we do something with that
extracted animal tag.

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:27.930
GRANT: Yes, and
so then we check.

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:31.290
If it's tracked
animal, then we upload

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:35.310
to the Firestore database,
in the collections,

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:39.621
with the animal name
and then with our data.

00:03:39.621 --> 00:03:41.800
MARTIN: OK, cool, so
that's a one liner

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:45.610
that just creates the
record in the database.

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:47.050
Love that.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:50.200
OK, cool, the code looks like
it's doing the right thing,

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:50.980
I think.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:52.030
But who knows?

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:55.285
Does it actually
work in real life?

00:03:55.285 --> 00:03:57.285
GRANT: Million dollar question.

00:03:57.285 --> 00:03:58.660
MARTIN: Another one, yes.

00:03:58.660 --> 00:03:59.830
GRANT: Let's deploy it.

00:04:02.812 --> 00:04:07.460
OK, so we have our handy
dandy deploy script.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:09.630
MARTIN: Oh, I love
those deploy scripts.

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:13.913
So this is-- you could have
typed out the full command.

00:04:13.913 --> 00:04:15.830
But it's often easier
to have a deploy script.

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:17.839
Also, this is kind of
the script that you

00:04:17.839 --> 00:04:21.019
would put at the end of
your CI/CD pipeline, right?

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:23.870
Typically, you
wouldn't type it in.

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:25.950
But your CI/CD system would--

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:28.470
whenever there's an
update to the main branch,

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:31.535
it would probably run
something like this.

00:04:31.535 --> 00:04:32.660
And I see you pulled it up.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:35.312
What happens here on
these lines, Grant?

00:04:35.312 --> 00:04:38.960
GRANT: Yeah, so we're going to
use gcloud functions deploy,

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:40.655
providing our function name.

00:04:40.655 --> 00:04:42.200
MARTIN: Mm-hmm.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:45.240
GRANT: And then we're
going to specify a runtime.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:49.430
In this case, we're
using nodejs10.

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:56.510
We could provide Python
3.7 or Ruby or even .NET.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:02.480
And then we're going to
specify a couple parameters

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:04.550
for the trigger.

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:10.390
We're going to trigger
whenever a new file is uploaded

00:05:10.390 --> 00:05:14.180
to the CritterWatcher's
bucket, and only

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:18.350
trigger this whenever the
object is finally uploaded.

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.900
MARTIN: So finalized
means the upload is done.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:22.010
Is that right?

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:23.340
GRANT: Yes.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:24.900
MARTIN: OK, cool.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.440
In the terminal layer, it looks
like your deployment script

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.330
finished.

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:31.830
Now, how do we
exercise this code?

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:34.380
How do we make it run to see if
it actually inserts something

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:36.490
into Firestore?

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:43.180
GRANT: Well, we can go and
trigger the function just using

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:44.680
the browser.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:50.890
MARTIN: Oh, cool, so you will
pretend to be a camera, huh?

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:51.510
GRANT: Yes.

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:52.010
MARTIN: OK.

00:05:52.010 --> 00:05:56.780
GRANT: So I mean,
so let's go and add

00:05:56.780 --> 00:06:01.430
some images to our
camera, into our bucket,

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:03.670
and see if our function works.

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:04.820
MARTIN: Great.

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:08.910
GRANT: So I'm going to just
drag and drop a couple files.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:11.090
MARTIN: Uh-huh, very good.

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:12.090
GRANT: And--

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:14.856
MARTIN: Oh, they all uploaded.

00:06:14.856 --> 00:06:16.650
GRANT: Very nice.

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:22.110
MARTIN: All right, so now
for each of these four files,

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:23.730
our analyze image
function should

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:25.920
have been triggered once.

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:28.780
And the analyzed image
inserts into Firestore.

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:31.230
So if everything
worked, we should

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:33.120
see four records in Firestore.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:34.392
Is that right?

00:06:34.392 --> 00:06:36.290
GRANT: Yes.

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:46.170
If we go check our database
and refresh and go see,

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:48.586
we should have some new records.

00:06:48.586 --> 00:06:52.650
MARTIN: Oh, I see
alpaca, dog, gecko.

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:54.420
Can you make the
font bigger, Grant?

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:57.725
GRANT: Yeah, let's go.

00:06:57.725 --> 00:07:00.330
MARTIN: Thank you.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:05.370
GRANT: We can see, all of
our images are classified.

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:06.510
MARTIN: Excellent.

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:08.730
So alpaca, there-- can we see?

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:11.230
What does the alpaca
photo look like?

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:13.200
GRANT: So, yeah, just
in the photos URL--

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:16.950
see?

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:18.050
A wild alpaca.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:20.700
MARTIN: Oh, look
at that [LAUGHS]..

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:23.460
So are we sure, though, that
this is an alpaca and not

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:24.840
a llama?

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:26.070
GRANT: Oh, yeah, I'm sure.

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:30.100
At least, that's what the
tour guides told me in Cusco,

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:31.560
in Peru.

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:33.780
MARTIN: Oh, really--
you took this picture?

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:35.400
GRANT: Yeah, I took the photo.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:37.770
And so I'm sure we
have the rights to it.

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:39.480
And we can use it.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:42.040
And you can even download it.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:43.320
MARTIN: Ah, excellent.

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:45.300
I will sleep a
lot better tonight

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:48.580
knowing that we haven't
violated somebody's copyright.

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:49.200
This is great.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:51.690
Also, I feel really good about--

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:54.443
we have a tour guide and we
have the Vision API both saying,

00:07:54.443 --> 00:07:55.110
it's an alpaca--

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:55.610
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:57.330
--and not a llama.

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:58.170
GRANT: Mm-hmm.

00:07:58.170 --> 00:07:59.260
MARTIN: OK, cool.

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:04.020
So this event-driven
function now works.

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:06.600
CritterWatch can use this.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:08.970
All the cameras will
call it implicitly

00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:12.747
when they upload images that
they're taking in the field.

00:08:12.747 --> 00:08:14.580
Now, CritterWatch is
really happy with this.

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:15.450
It just runs.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:17.550
It's not a lot of code.

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:20.310
But eventually, they
discover that they

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:24.750
will want to have a REST API
in front of this database.

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:27.000
And the reason is that they
will have applications--

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:29.520
web applications,
mobile applications--

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:31.560
where you can see photos of--

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:33.720
I want to see all
the alpacas that

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:37.049
have gone through my backyard.

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:40.169
So they want to put that REST
API in front of the database.

00:08:40.169 --> 00:08:43.980
They also want to be able
to do traffic splitting, so

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:45.855
canary deployments,
like, they deploy

00:08:45.855 --> 00:08:47.760
a new version of the REST API.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.100
And they want to be
able to then send only,

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:53.820
for example, 1% or 10% of the
traffic to the new version.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.550
They also, of course, want
to put their own domain

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:58.620
name in front of this REST API.

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:00.810
So it would be
CritterWatchAPI.com

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:02.790
or something.

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:06.360
And finally, their CTO
has said that Containers

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:08.610
is strategic for them.

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:11.400
Now again, we have
Cloud Functions,

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:12.630
and we have Cloud Run.

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:14.490
Given these requirements,
what should they

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:18.210
use to build their
REST API, Grant?

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:19.930
GRANT: Good question, Martin.

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:21.980
Well, looking at
the selection grid,

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:25.170
it looks like they should
probably use Cloud Run.

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:28.080
I mean, they could
use Cloud Functions.

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:30.700
People say, Cloud
Functions is easier.

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:33.930
But it doesn't support traffic
splitting or custom demands,

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:35.740
for example.

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:40.450
So looking at the
API rows of the grid,

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:44.610
the check boxes clearly
show that Cloud Run

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:46.510
makes a lot of sense.

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:48.420
I mean, we could use App Engine.

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:50.130
But they wanted Containers.

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:51.830
So let's go with Cloud Run.

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:54.510
MARTIN: OK, Cloud Run it is.

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:58.160
What would the code look
like for this API, Grant?

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:00.980
GRANT: Now, let's go
to our code editor.

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:04.370
MARTIN: All right, so we're
looking at another index.js.

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:06.395
You like Node, Grant, right?

00:10:06.395 --> 00:10:08.030
GRANT: Yes, I love Node.

00:10:08.030 --> 00:10:10.520
MARTIN: [LAUGHS] Me, too--

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:11.960
I'm happy you picked Node.

00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:13.730
All right, so what's
happening here?

00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:16.340
This seems to be a little
more at the top of the file

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:19.140
than we saw in the
previous example.

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:22.280
GRANT: Yeah, so whereas
with Cloud Functions,

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:24.680
you have to just
write your code,

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:28.220
and you don't worry about your
server as much, with Cloud Run,

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:30.890
you have to take
care of everything,

00:10:30.890 --> 00:10:35.800
like listening to your port and
setting up a server yourself.

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:37.470
MARTIN: Ah, I see.

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:40.070
So the express
framework-- that is

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:43.350
a library for
listening to HTTP calls

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:44.870
that are coming in, right?

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:45.738
GRANT: Yes.

00:10:45.738 --> 00:10:46.280
MARTIN: Cool.

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:50.210
GRANT: And so we go and
set up express here.

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:54.830
You set up an app
listening to port 8080.

00:10:54.830 --> 00:10:58.666
And we're going to also
use the Firestore API.

00:10:58.666 --> 00:11:01.120
And so--

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:02.145
MARTIN: All right.

00:11:02.145 --> 00:11:02.645
GRANT: Yes.

00:11:02.645 --> 00:11:05.300
MARTIN: Very good, so there
is a little more setup

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:08.460
there in the beginning than for
Cloud Functions, it looks like.

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:11.807
But then the real action
starts on line 11.

00:11:11.807 --> 00:11:12.890
Could you walk us through?

00:11:12.890 --> 00:11:15.520
What happens there
on line 11, Grant?

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:16.720
GRANT: Sure.

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:22.750
So whenever we receive a
get request to animals,

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:25.710
we'll go and take
the animal name.

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:29.410
We'll find it in our
Firestore database

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:33.920
if we did find something,
if it's not empty.

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:37.390
Then we're just going to get
the actual sighting and return

00:11:37.390 --> 00:11:42.940
status success with the
sighting from the database.

00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:45.370
Otherwise, we're going
to just say, 404--

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:47.310
we couldn't find this animal--

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:51.640
status failure, and say,
the animal wasn't found.

00:11:51.640 --> 00:11:55.270
MARTIN: OK, and animalName,
up there on line 11,

00:11:55.270 --> 00:11:56.590
has a colon in front of it.

00:11:56.590 --> 00:11:58.510
What does that mean?

00:11:58.510 --> 00:12:03.070
GRANT: Yes, so this means
it's a parameter for the URL.

00:12:03.070 --> 00:12:07.668
So it could be animals/fish
or animals/hummingbird.

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:11.560
MARTIN: Ah, OK.

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:15.610
So this is-- OK, you would
put your animal name in there.

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:16.690
Got it.

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:19.210
All right, well, I don't
know about you, Grant.

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:22.425
But I feel another million
question coming up.

00:12:22.425 --> 00:12:23.800
GRANT: They come
up pretty often.

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:26.110
MARTIN: [LAUGHS] Yes.

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:27.250
